\formshade{\*\pgdscno0}\paperh15840\paperw12240\margl1296\margr1296\margt1152\margb1152\sectd\sbknone\sectunlocked1\pgndec\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1296\margrsxn1296\margtsxn1152\margbsxn1152\ftnbj\ftnstart1\ftnrstcont\ftnnar\aenddoc\aftnrstcont\aftnstart1\aftnnrlc
\pgndec\pard\plain \s51\sl100\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa300\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\brdrcf3\brsp80{\*\brdrb\brdlncol3\brdlnin0\brdlnout20\brdlndist0}\brsp80\ltrpar\cf6\expnd1\expndtw5\b\kerning1\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs52\ab\loch\f7\fs44\qc{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Method for Verifying Places in the Notitia Dignitatum}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\b\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Core principle. }{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
No place name may enter the gazetteer unless it actually occurs in the Latin text of the Notitia Dignitatum. Pleiades and other modern resources are used only to identify and locate names already attested in the source, never to introduce new ones.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Olof Hesselmark 2026-10-04}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
olof.hesselmark@mac.com}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  1.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The source text is primary}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The starting point is Otto Seeck\uc3 \u8217\'e2\'80\'99s transcription of the Notitia Dignitatum. For every possible place we record the exact name form occurring in the text, the source line, chapter and administrative context, and the province, region, military unit, or office where relevant.\uc1 }
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The Latin source text is never altered. Inflected forms and manuscript spellings remain preserved in sourcePlaceName and in the full source line.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  2.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360{\rtlch \ltrch
 }{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Latin normalization is kept separate}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Place names often occur in grammatically inflected forms, for example Aquileiae \uc3 \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Aquileia, Altino \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Altinum, and Amidae \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Amida.\uc1 }
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A separate normalized Latin form is therefore used for map display. This does not replace the form in the source. The data model distinguishes: Notitia form \uc3 \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 normalized Latin name \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Pleiades identity \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 modern name. The modern place name can later be added as another independent field.\uc1 }
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  3.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A name is not the same thing as a place}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A major methodological finding is that the same textual form may refer to several different geographical places. Examples include Constantia / Constantiae, Alta, Ad Herculem, Novae, and Brigantia.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A name string must therefore never function as the unique identifier. The real unit of analysis is the individual occurrence in the text: Notitia occurrence \uc3 \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 historical place \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Pleiades ID. Source line, chapter, province, and administrative context are used to determine which geographical entity is intended.\uc1 }
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  4.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch
 }{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Context is more important than name similarity}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A Pleiades match is not accepted merely because the names resemble one another. Each candidate is tested against province, diocese, military command, neighbouring places, river and road systems, frontier organization, and function such as fort, harbour, fleet station, or settlement.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
This procedure has repeatedly exposed false matches. Brecantia was initially associated with Confluentes/Koblenz; the administrative context instead identifies it as Brigantium/Bregenz. Dubris had been matched to a river; the Saxon Shore context shows that it is Portus Dubris, Dover. Foro Fuluiensi had also been connected with a river, but the correct identification is Forum Fulvii Valentinum.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  5.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch
 }{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Pleiades is an authority register, not an infallible answer key}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Pleiades is the principal external gazetteer, but its entries are not accepted automatically. For each candidate we examine the Pleiades ID, ancient name, description, feature type, coordinates, and geographical context.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Sometimes the historical identity is secure although the exact location is not known. In such cases we retain the Pleiades ID but do not invent coordinates. A securely identified but unlocated ancient place is preferable to a precise but incorrect point on the map.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  6.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Other ancient and modern sources are used for difficult cases}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
When Pleiades alone is insufficient, other evidence is consulted, including the Tabula Peutingeriana, ancient itineraries, historical gazetteers, archaeological publications, limes studies, and modern historical-geographical scholarship.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
These sources can confirm both textual forms and proposed identifications. For example, Siosta could be linked with Sostra through comparison with the Tabula Peutingeriana, while Bintha could be identified with Birtha, with the transmitted Notitia form Bintha preserved unchanged in the source field.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  7.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Non-place entities are separated from point locations}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Not every geographically suggestive expression is a settlement or fort. The database distinguishes settlements, forts, stations, harbours, rivers, provinces, regions, limes districts, military units, administrative expressions, and erroneous extractions.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Examples that should not be displayed as ordinary point locations include Galliarum, Dalmatiae, Scythiam, Diocletiana, and Septimani. This prevents administrative or grammatical forms from being converted into fictitious map points.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  8.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Uncertainty is explicitly classified}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Confirmed: the identification is strongly supported by name, context, and geography. Probable: the identification is very likely, but some part of the evidence remains incomplete. Ambiguous / occurrence split required: the same form refers to more than one location or cannot safely be resolved globally. Unlocated: the historical place is identified, but its precise geographical position is unknown. Rejected / non-place: the earlier match was incorrect, or the expression is not a point location.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Uncertainty is therefore preserved in the data instead of being hidden.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  9.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Earlier identifications remain revisable}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The method is iterative. Earlier matches are corrected when stronger evidence becomes available. Examples include Brecantia \uc3 \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Brigantium, Dubris \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Portus Dubris, Foro Fuluiensi \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Forum Fulvii Valentinum, Hadrianopolis Haemimonti \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Hadrianopolis/Edirne, and Siosta \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 Sostra.\uc1 }
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The historical source fields remain unchanged. Only our interpretation and geographical identification are revised.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  10.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch
 }{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The map is the result of the source criticism}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Only records that satisfy the data model are displayed as geographical places. The map label uses the normalized Latin name, while the inspector can display the exact Notitia form, the full Latin source line, administrative context, Pleiades identification, coordinates, and later the modern place name.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The map is therefore not merely an illustration. It is the visible result of the philological and geographical verification process.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  11.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Practical workflow}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033{\i\rtlch \ltrch\loch
extract a name from Seeck \uc3 \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 verify that the occurrence is genuinely geographical \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 normalize the Latin inflected form \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 establish the occurrence context \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 search for Pleiades candidates \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 test province and geography \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 consult additional ancient or modern sources where necessary \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 assign a confidence level \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 add a Pleiades ID and coordinates only when justified \u8594\'e2\'86\'92 preserve the Latin source text unchanged.\uc1 }
\par \pard\plain \s1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af15\dbch\af16\afs28\ab\loch\f7\fs28{\listtext\pard\plain  12.\tab}\ls1 \li1080\ri0\lin1080\rin0\fi-360\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Central methodological principle}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033\qc{\b\rtlch \ltrch\loch
We do not match words to Pleiades. We identify geographical occurrences in the Notitia Dignitatum and then link those occurrences to historical places.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar\sl276\slmult1{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\sb0\sa200\cf0\hich\af14\langfe1033\dbch\af16\afs22\lang1025\loch\f6\fs21\lang1033\qc{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
